        <p>Chart week is the Billboard Hot 100 week that contains May 1 that year.</p>
        <p>Week of run says which week of the song's time at number one that was, like 3 of 6.</p>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from this site's weekly Hot 100 file, and the story lines are credited in Sources.</p>

        <h2 id="answer">The Short Answer: Who Was on Top Each Year</h2>
        <p>For May 1 in music history, a different record was number one in every year from 1970 to 1979.</p>
        <p>That is the Hot 100 chart week that includes May 1.</p>
        <p>Here is what the ten weeks show at a glance:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The ten songs spent {{TOTAL_WEEKS}} weeks at number one in total.</li>
          <li>"Night Fever" had the most weeks, with eight.</li>
          <li>"Joy to the World" and "The First Time Ever I Saw Your Face" came next, with six each.</li>
          <li>"Tie a Yellow Ribbon Round the Ole Oak Tree" had four, and "ABC" and "TSOP" had two each.</li>
          <li>The other four songs held number one for a single week.</li>
          <li>{{N_FIRST}} of the ten songs were in the first week of their run, {{N_MID}} were in the middle and {{N_LAST}} was in the last week.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Want Your Own Week?</h3>
        <p>The <a href="/pages/birthday-number-one">Birthday #1 Song Finder</a> does this for any 1970s birthday, not only May 1 in music history.</p>
        <p>Enter the date and it shows the song that was on top that week.</p>

        <h2 id="method">How the May 1 Week Was Picked</h2>
        <p>Billboard chart weeks do not line up with the calendar, so there is no single week called May 1.</p>
        <p>This May 1 in music history page uses the weekly Hot 100 week that contains May 1 each year.</p>
        <p>In 1971 and 1976 that week began on May 1 itself, and in the other eight years it began a few days before:</p>
        <ul>
{{DATE_LIST}}
        </ul>

        <h3>Where the Numbers Come From</h3>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from the weekly Hot 100 file on this site.</p>
        <p>The story line for each song is a fact from that song's Wikipedia page.</p>
        <p>Anything that could not be confirmed against those sources was left out.</p>
        <p>All ten runs were unbroken.</p>
        <p>So "week of run" counts the weeks since the song first reached number one.</p>

        <h3>Three Songs You May Have Seen Before</h3>
        <p>Three of these ten songs are also on the <a href="/blog/trivia/april-17-in-music-history/">April 17 in music history</a> list.</p>
        <p>"Joy to the World," "The First Time Ever I Saw Your Face" and "Night Fever" were all still on top the week that contains May 1.</p>
        <p>"Night Fever" is on the <a href="/blog/trivia/march-20-in-music-history/">March 20 in music history</a> list as well.</p>
        <p>Every calendar date left now shares at least three songs with an earlier list, so they stay here.</p>

        <h2 id="early">May 1 in Music History, 1970 to 1972: The Jackson 5, Seven Voices and a Slow Folk Song</h2>
        <p>May 1 in music history opened the decade with a Jackson 5 single in its first week on top.</p>
        <p>Next came a Three Dog Night singalong halfway through a six-week run.</p>
        <p>Then came Roberta Flack, at the same point in a six-week run of her own.</p>

        <h3>1970: "ABC" by The Jackson 5</h3>
        <p>Motown's team The Corporation wrote and produced "ABC."</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it was recorded in December 1969.</p>
        <p>The session musicians went uncredited, in line with Motown policy at the time.</p>
        <p>It was also number one on the Best Selling Soul Singles chart for four weeks.</p>
        <p>It was nominated for a Grammy, and it entered the Grammy Hall of Fame in 2016.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows two weeks at number one, from April 25 to May 2, 1970.</p>
        <p>The Beatles' "Let It Be" was on top just before it, and The Guess Who's "American Woman" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains May 1 was the first of two.</p>
        <p>The song is number 15 on the 1970 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>More of the group's hits are on the <a href="/blog/artists/jackson-5-songs/">Jackson 5 songs</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1971: "Joy to the World" by Three Dog Night</h3>
        <p>Hoyt Axton wrote "Joy to the World."</p>
        <p>Three Dog Night first released it on their fourth studio album, Naturally, in November 1970.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says all seven members of the band sang on the record, not only the three main vocalists.</p>
        <p>Chuck Negron felt the band needed a "silly song" to help them work together again.</p>
        <p>The RIAA certified the single Gold on April 9, 1971.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows six weeks at number one, from April 17 to May 22, 1971.</p>
        <p>The Temptations' "Just My Imagination" was on top before it, and The Rolling Stones' "Brown Sugar" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains May 1 was the third of six.</p>
        <p>The song is number 1 on the 1971 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>The full story is on the <a href="/blog/songs/joy-to-the-world-three-dog-night/">Joy to the World</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1972: "The First Time Ever I Saw Your Face" by Roberta Flack</h3>
        <p>Ewan MacColl wrote "The First Time Ever I Saw Your Face," and Peggy Seeger sang the original.</p>
        <p>Roberta Flack recorded it in February 1969 for her debut album, First Take.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says her version was much slower than Seeger's.</p>
        <p>The song won Grammy Awards for Record of the Year and Song of the Year.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows six weeks at number one, from April 15 to May 20, 1972.</p>
        <p>America's "A Horse with No Name" was on top before it, and The Chi-Lites' "Oh Girl" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains May 1 was the third of six.</p>
        <p>The song is number 1 on the 1972 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it topped Billboard's 1972 year-end list as well.</p>

        <h2 id="middle">May 1 in Music History, 1973 to 1975: A Yellow Ribbon, the Soul Train Theme and a Very Long Title</h2>
        <p>May 1 in music history in the middle of the decade brought the top-selling single of 1973.</p>
        <p>A TV theme followed in the last week of its run.</p>
        <p>Then came a country crossover with a title of record length.</p>

        <h3>1973: "Tie a Yellow Ribbon Round the Ole Oak Tree" by Dawn featuring Tony Orlando</h3>
        <p>Irwin Levine and L. Russell Brown wrote "Tie a Yellow Ribbon Round the Ole Oak Tree."</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says Brown got the idea from a Pete Hamill newspaper column, after Reader's Digest reprinted it.</p>
        <p>The single came out on February 19, 1973.</p>
        <p>It sold 3 million records in the US in three weeks.</p>
        <p>It was the top-selling single of 1973 in both the US and the UK.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows four weeks at number one, from April 21 to May 12, 1973.</p>
        <p>Vicki Lawrence's "The Night the Lights Went Out in Georgia" was on top before it.</p>
        <p>Stevie Wonder's "You Are the Sunshine of My Life" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains May 1 was the second of four.</p>
        <p>The song is number 1 on the 1973 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1974: "TSOP (The Sound of Philadelphia)" by MFSB and The Three Degrees</h3>
        <p>Gamble and Huff wrote "TSOP" as the theme for the TV show Soul Train.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says Soul Train's Don Cornelius would not allow the single to name the show.</p>
        <p>It came out in the US on February 6, 1974.</p>
        <p>It was the first television theme song to reach number one on the Hot 100.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows two weeks at number one, from April 20 to April 27, 1974.</p>
        <p>Elton John's "Bennie and the Jets" was on top before it, and Grand Funk's "The Loco-Motion" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains May 1 was the second and last.</p>
        <p>The song is number 7 on the 1974 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>More of that Philadelphia sound is in <a href="/blog/genres/70s-soul-music/">70s soul music</a>.</p>

        <h3>1975: "(Hey Won't You Play) Another Somebody Done Somebody Wrong Song" by B. J. Thomas</h3>
        <p>Larry Butler and Chips Moman wrote the song, and B. J. Thomas recorded it.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it debuted at number 99 on the Hot 100 on February 1, 1975.</p>
        <p>It reached number one on April 26 and became Thomas's second number one single.</p>
        <p>It also went to number one on the Hot Country Songs chart.</p>
        <p>It was the longest title of any single to top the Hot 100 up to that time.</p>
        <p>It won the 1976 Grammy for Best Country Song.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of April 26, 1975.</p>
        <p>The Elton John Band's "Philadelphia Freedom" was on top before it, and Tony Orlando and Dawn's "He Don't Love You (Like I Love You)" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains May 1 was the first and only one.</p>
        <p>The song is number 18 on the 1975 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h2 id="late">May 1 in Music History, 1976 to 1979: A Debut Duo, a Louisiana Memory, Night Fever and Blondie</h2>
        <p>May 1 in music history closed the decade with three one-week number ones.</p>
        <p>The Bellamy Brothers and Glen Campbell each had a week, and so did Blondie.</p>
        <p>In between, the Bee Gees were near the end of an eight-week run.</p>

        <h3>1976: "Let Your Love Flow" by The Bellamy Brothers</h3>
        <p>Larry E. Williams wrote "Let Your Love Flow," and Phil Gernhard and Tony Scotti produced it.</p>
        <p>It was the debut single by the country duo the Bellamy Brothers.</p>
        <p>They recorded it in late 1975, and it came out in January 1976.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says Neil Diamond's drummer suggested the song as right for the Bellamys.</p>
        <p>It reached number one on the Hot 100 dated May 1, 1976, and spent five weeks on top in Germany.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of May 1, 1976.</p>
        <p>Johnnie Taylor's "Disco Lady" was on top before it, and John Sebastian's "Welcome Back" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains May 1 was the first and only one.</p>
        <p>The song is number 36 on the 1976 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>More crossover hits are on the <a href="/blog/genres/70s-country-songs/">70s country songs</a> list.</p>

        <h3>1977: "Southern Nights" by Glen Campbell</h3>
        <p>Allen Toussaint wrote and first recorded "Southern Nights."</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it came from his childhood memories of visiting relatives in the Louisiana backwoods.</p>
        <p>When Glen Campbell heard Toussaint's version, he identified with the lyrics right away.</p>
        <p>Campbell's record used a guitar lick he had learned from his friend Jerry Reed.</p>
        <p>It reached number one on three separate US charts, including two weeks on the country chart.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of April 30, 1977.</p>
        <p>Thelma Houston's "Don't Leave Me This Way" was on top before it, and the Eagles' "Hotel California" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains May 1 was the first and only one.</p>
        <p>The song is number 22 on the 1977 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1978: "Night Fever" by the Bee Gees</h3>
        <p>Barry Gibb sang lead on "Night Fever."</p>
        <p>Billboard described its smooth falsetto lead over a jumping disco beat.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says its string intro was inspired by Percy Faith's "Theme from A Summer Place."</p>
        <p>It replaced Andy Gibb's "(Love Is) Thicker Than Water" at number one.</p>
        <p>Yvonne Elliman's "If I Can't Have You" replaced it.</p>
        <p>It also topped the UK Singles Chart for two weeks, the group's third UK number one.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows eight weeks at number one, from March 18 to May 6, 1978.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains May 1 was the seventh of eight.</p>
        <p>The song is number 2 on the 1978 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>The full story is on the <a href="/blog/songs/night-fever/">Night Fever</a> page.</p>

        <h3>1979: "Heart of Glass" by Blondie</h3>
        <p>Debbie Harry and Chris Stein wrote "Heart of Glass," and Mike Chapman produced it.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says they wrote an early version called "Once I Had a Love" in 1974 and 1975.</p>
        <p>The record was built around a Roland CR-78 drum machine.</p>
        <p>For the single, Chapman remixed the track to push the bass drum further forward.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia calls it one of the first rock and disco fusion hits.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of April 28, 1979.</p>
        <p>Amii Stewart's "Knock on Wood" was on top before it, and Peaches &amp; Herb's "Reunited" came next.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains May 1 was the first and only one.</p>
        <p>The song is number 18 on the 1979 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>Blondie's roots are covered in <a href="/blog/genres/70s-punk-rock/">70s punk rock</a>.</p>

